## 8. What I did not build

* **No parallelism.** Selection runs in one process with `pool=None`, exactly as the archived runs did. `SELECTION_WORKERS` in the sealed runner is 1 and I did not touch it. A four-worker path would be the single largest remaining speed-up on a cold panel and is untouched.
* **No arm-level compute selection.** `--arms` filters reporting only. Computing `ORACLE_SET` without `UNILATERAL`, or either without `BASELINE`, is not possible under the sealed definitions and was not attempted.
* **No third variant.** The seam is built and the two required variants are registered. Nothing was screened, ranked or recommended.
* **No new panels.** Only `FULL20` and `SCREEN2`. There is no way to run an arbitrary subset of anchors by id; a new panel is a small edit to `harness/panel.py`, not a command-line option.
* **No statistical inference.** No confidence intervals, no hypothesis tests, no bootstrap. The `dist` command reports order statistics and counts, nothing more. Adding inference would make this an experiment rather than an instrument.
* **No cache management.** No eviction, no size cap, no locking. Two harness processes writing the same cache key concurrently is not guarded against. 481 MB for four tapes.
* **No verification of the sealed physics itself.** The harness proves that the bytes it runs are the bytes that produced the archive. It says nothing about whether those bytes are physically correct, and the `MARGIN_Q` results are a diagnostic of a provisioning rule, not a finding about it.
* **No repair of the pre-existing test baseline.** The 2026-09-09 harness audit in this workspace found 6 failing tests and one non-terminating Stage-C test in `tests/physics_v025` and `tests/stagec_v025`. I did not touch those suites and did not fix them. The harness deliberately does not import this workspace's `src/mcrl` at all, so those failures neither affect nor are affected by anything here — but they remain open.
* **No cross-machine claim.** All timings are from this machine, one process, `nice -n 15`. The archived ladder run of the same panel took 1 598.9 s on its machine; that is not a before/after comparison and is not presented as one.
* **No receipt immutability beyond SHA-256.** Receipts are plain files. Nothing signs them or prevents them being edited.
* **No migration of this workspace's `src/` to the sealed version.** They differ in nine physics modules. I vendored rather than replaced, deliberately, because replacing a sealed tree is exactly the thing this brief forbids. Anyone running the repo's own tests is still running the older physics; that divergence is real and unresolved.
At 30.08 seconds, **81.74%** of the converged first-improvement unilateral gain is available under `SEALED` and **87.85%** under `MARGIN_Q` (against the archived best-improvement endpoints: **89.39%** and **89.60%**).

# Anytime unilateral search — V0.25 twenty-anchor panel

`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM`. No learner, training run, or policy run was performed. No sealed constant, threshold, sign, seed, horizon, price, service guard, acceptance rule, sealed artifact, or frozen manifest was changed.

## Decision

Most of the prize is available before the deadline under both provisioning rules. By 20 seconds the first-improvement search has already obtained 69.51% (`SEALED`) and 77.03% (`MARGIN_Q`) of its eventual pooled gain; by 30.08 seconds those fractions are 81.74% and 87.85%. Therefore the proposed argument—*the converged exact search misses the interval, so a learned method may claim the within-interval win*—fails on this panel. A learned method would need to beat the actual anytime incumbent at the same wall-clock budget, not the legal base configuration or a convergence certificate time.

First-improvement changed the local-search path and the fixed point on all 20 anchors under both rules. Its converged pooled EE is 8.13% above the archived best-improvement endpoint under `SEALED` and 1.62% above it under `MARGIN_Q`. Accordingly, every prize fraction below is shown against both endpoints where relevant.

## Method and timing contract

The legal nearest-eligible base assignment is the incumbent from time zero. Users and their legal options retain sealed deterministic order. Alternatives are evaluated in ordered microbatches of 16 using one persistent `StepEvaluator`; when a batch returns, the first strict `F = B - eta_ref E` improvement satisfying the unchanged baseline served-count guard is committed immediately and later alternatives for that user are not evaluated. Thus a checkpoint receives only a move whose evaluation completed by that timestamp, never a candidate credited with hindsight. The microbatch is the sealed dense evaluator's native vectorized path; scalar calls were piloted and rejected as an implementation defect after taking 176.84 seconds on the first anchor.

The stopwatch starts before base construction and `_legal_options`; it includes all configuration/candidate construction, physical selection evaluation, and cache misses. The immutable exogenous world tape is the input to a decision and was loaded once per world from the standing content-addressed cache; its load times remain in each receipt. The 48-boundary committed evaluation is run only after timing to measure the true action endpoint and cannot affect the search path. The final complete no-move sweep is executed only as a separate certification probe; its evaluations are excluded from the anytime and joint caches.

Runs used one process via `nice -n 15` on an Intel Core Ultra 7 265KF (20 logical CPUs, one thread per core). `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS`, and `NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS` were all pinned to 1. The interpreter was `/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover/.venv/bin/python`; `PYTHONPATH=src`. The runtime reported no active native threadpool. The sealed manifest is `d5e94654510225aaf6ca7f8422ecd12ba64e8f3b8690c55a55d791063db8b916`; the anytime implementation SHA-256 is `003bdee1df908fddf652f2f8069c31a6297a7f7178bbb83338d09b3f4add9928`.

